# Tell GitHub Linguist to highlight .br files as Swift (closest approximation).
# Boring is inspired by Swift and Python; Swift gives the best keyword coverage
# (let, var, guard, struct, throws, defer, nil, etc.).
*.br linguist-language=Swift

# Shell scripts must stay LF -- a `#!/usr/bin/env bash` shebang breaks
# ("bash\r: No such file or directory") the moment CRLF reaches a Linux
# container (e.g. tools/docker-nvcc-check mounts the repo straight into
# a Docker Linux image). Without this, Windows' core.autocrlf=true keeps
# re-introducing CRLF on every checkout, silently undoing any one-off fix.
*.sh text eol=lf

# try_wrap_double_handling's fixture is read with fs.read/readBytes/readLines and its
# .expected output hard-codes an exact byte count (33) and line count (3) for it. Windows'
# core.autocrlf=true would otherwise turn its LF endings into CRLF on checkout, growing it
# to 35 bytes and breaking that test with no code change involved -- pin it to LF so the
# fixture's on-disk bytes are identical on every platform.
tests/cases/fixtures/try_wrap_fixture.txt text eol=lf
